nguage layer is a Java layer here we  use one of our other Wolfram language  technology called as jailing which  communicates with the database and also  with the top Wolfram language layer the  part that communicates with the database  which is the Java layer here does it  through this JDBC that's why we have in  our database link package you have the  JDBC drivers that are bundled up here so  once you hit shift enter with the  command the command just goes to this  Java layer and then it gets executed it  gets the data from the database and  comes back to Wolfram language layer  where it can be used it's like a list of  data and where it can be used with other  Wolfram language functions like plots or  for numerical analysis or data modeling  etc now because we have commands that  you can use in word Straw sequel and  Wolfram language it is a very convenient  tool like if you are not very familiar  with sequel language you can just follow  the Wolfram language commands which are  pretty into intuitive and it's easy to  use and on the other hand let's say if  you're not very familiar with Wolfram  language but you're good at sequel that  works fine too  so there is there are functions where  you can actually provide just the raw  sequel command and that works as well  now let's see where this database link  sits in the installation so if you  evaluate this command here it opens up  this directory which is called database  link and if you have Mathematica  installed you can evaluate this  and this will open up this particular  directory here and inside this you can  see that there is a Java directory and a  kernel directory and that's where the  source code for database links its so  this is all the Java classes and this is  all the Wolfram language side of it so  if you want to do a little experiment on  your own you can also modify this source  files little bit and then reload the  package and see what effect it has now  let's see how we make connections to the  database so since this is a package  within Wolfram language it needs to be  loaded in your current kernel session  first so to do that we issue this  command called needs database link this  will load the package into your current  session now to do that there as I  already said that there is there are  some JDBC drivers that are bundled with  database link now we have to make sure  that these drivers are on the path or on  the class path to be recognizable by  database link by default they are  already added to the path so you don't  have to do add to class path or anything  to make them available on the path  however if you find that there is a  database that you want to use for which  a JDBC driver is not included in the  package you can always download that and  add it up in several locations within  your Mathematica installation one such  location is this dollar user base  directory and another location is this  base directory and all the other current  drivers actually sit in the dollar  installation directory and this is  another place where you can add it as  now that we have loaded the package we  want to connect to a database called as  demo this demo database comes with Mac  Marika it's a very lightweight database  built upon each sequel DB and you don't  have to configure anything to to work  with this database and where you open a  connection to this database is using  this open sequel connection command and  just give the name of the database  so once you do that you get this sequel  connection object it is basically a Java  object so if I do connection full form  you can see that in generally this is  just a Java object that it created for  communication between Wolfram language  and the database now at any point if you  want to know what are the current open  connections you can evaluate sequel  connections I already opened a few  connections so that you can see the  output of this one that there are four  connections to currently open and at any  point if you want to see what are the  properties of a particular open  connection you can use sequel connection  information now this sequel connection  information gives a lot of information  so I'm suppressing the output here by  putting a semicolon but you can always  take a look at its short form so these  are the properties for which it will  tell you what are the values set for  that connection and there is a about 167  of them now if you want to close the  connection close equal connection and  just give the connection object and that  will close the current open connection  now in the previous example we saw that  we're opening a connection to an already  built in database and that is already  configured and we don't have to put a  server name or a username or password or  any other properties that we would  rather use in a connection string now  let's say if you want to use those all  those properties and open a connection  then you can provide some extra  arguments to open sequel connection for  example here you can see that I'm  providing the name of the database  engine and providing the database to  which I want to open a connection and a  name and a user name so let's try that  opens a connection here another example  that more commonly used is with my  sequel connection now this will this my  sequel database it's on my local machine  and it's called test DB and the machine  is localhost and I'm providing a  username and password now my sequel  connection J this is the name of the  configuration file that sits in database  link now this configuration file  consists of all the connection details  in it if you go to the database link  user guide you will be able to see how  to confirm it how to configure a  connection how to make this  configuration file and put it at a  location which is recognizable by  database link now here if you notice for  password I have a dollar prompt in there  and which means that when I evaluate  this it should prompt up a window for me  to enter the password rather than just  typing the password in the notebook here  so that's the prompt we get I'm gonna  enter my password here and that makes a  connection to test DB so this dollar  prompt is useful if you don't want the  password to show up for C  confidentiality reasons  now there is also a graphical user  interface which is built into database  link and this is a very very basic form  of the interface it's it's something  that beginners can use and to get  familiar with you know how database link  is working and how to select something  from table and do very simple operations  let me show you an example here so you  can evaluate this database Explorer  command this is the window that comes up  and I can make a connection to any of  the configurations that I've already  saved now this will show me what tables  the demo database has and what columns  it has and if I so currently the table  selected a sample table one and the  column that selected is entry if I just  click on result it's going to select  everything from entry now there is also  a connection tool which is called the  opens sequel connection tool and that is  just the connection part of the previous  database Explorer that we saw so if  let's say if you want if you want to  establish a connection more frequently  to a certain database and you want to  save its configuration in a file you can  do that and have your connections listed  here so once you open this click on  let's say demo and connect it's just  going to give you a connection object  same as by putting all the arguments  within open secure connection now let's  go to the security and authentication  part which is one of the most important  parts when you are working with a  database so if your database supports  SSL it is possible for database link to  talk to the database through SSL there  are some steps that need to be followed  and the four major ones are listed here  the first one is to get a certificate of  authority and then generate a trust  stork file then you need to configure  the Java that you are using to use that  trust or file and then configure your  opens  connection to use the ssl let me open up  the documentation and show you a little  more detail about this one so this is a  more comprehensive documentation on  using ssl and here's an example using  Postgres sequel so here we are creating  a certificate called CA dot c RT it's a  sample certificate and then we are just  creating a trust store file here now you  want to add that trust or file to your  current Java JVM arguments and that's  done that which is what is done here and  when you're doing the open sequel  connection you can either provide this  as a query parameter where an SSL is set  to true or you can use properties option  within open sequel connection and set  SSL to true now this will let you use as  a cell with database link but you have  to be sure that your database supports  SSL in the first place now there are  several tips that you can use to make  your operations secure you can use a  connection configuration file and you  can always encode that file so that the  connection configuration is not readable  easily readable by any third party and  you can use dollar prompt as I showed  you before  and if you're using a web server you can  also use the authentication mechanism  provided by the web server and  Mathematica will retrieve the password  from the server and the details for all  of this is available in the database  user guide here are some resources for  you to go through these are the  connections parts of database  connections and resources documentation  is available here and it tells you all  the different functions that relate to  making and configuring a connection  there is also a documentation on  database Explorer it's a little more  detail than what we saw and also  includes the  recommendation on connection tool and  how to query the database using this  tool so this brings us to the end of  part one next we will be going on to  part two which will show how to use  different commands to query the database

